With DLSS (0161), the surfaces the game draws into before its 3D scene in a frame, such as shadow maps, are drawn at
the smallest whole resolution scale that keeps the scene's detail instead of the full one: 2x for Performance at a 4x
render resolution, 3x for Quality and Balanced. A surface is a pitch and a sample count, so a pass's color and depth
targets go together; it qualifies after three frames that drew it before the scene and never after it, and no draw
after the scene samples a texture resolved from it (a later resolve to the same memory replaces that texture). It
stays at the full scale for the session once one does. Changes apply between frames only. Its draws get the host
viewport and scissor scaled about the EDRAM origin like the scene's, without jitter, and at each of its resolves
scene_edram_stretch copies the resolve rectangle from the EDRAM, each sample from the nearest smaller-scale pixel, into
a buffer the size of the EDRAM at the same addresses (the DLSS staging buffer, made that big), which Resolve copies
from instead of the EDRAM (copy_source); clears still go to the EDRAM. Every format, sample count and depth works,
as the copy is raw. Nothing after the scene changes scale: post-processing, the HUD, menus and the presenter keep the
full Render resolution. The log gets the scale domains when DLSS starts, the surfaces drawn smaller or kept whenever
that changes, and the GPU targets line shows each target's host size at the scale its draws used.
d3d12_dlss_pre_scene_scale (on) turns it off. Builds on 0161.

